.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_ARENA 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_new_arena ,
.Nm xft_arena_alloc ,
.Nm xft_destroy_arena ,
.Nm xft_arena_checkpoint ,
.Nm xft_arena_rewind ,
.Nm xft_arena_rewind_clean
.Nd bump allocator over large page mappings
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In alloc.h
.Ft t_arena
.Fn xft_new_arena void
.Ft t_any
.Fn xft_arena_alloc "t_arena *allocator" "t_size size" "t_size align"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_destroy_arena "t_arena *alloc"
.Ft t_arena_checkpoint
.Fn xft_arena_checkpoint "const t_arena *arena"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_arena_rewind "t_arena *arena" "t_arena_checkpoint checkpoint"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_arena_rewind_clean "t_arena *arena" "t_arena_checkpoint checkpoint"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An arena hands out memory by bumping an offset inside a chain of large
mappings and frees it all at once, either by destroying the arena or by
rewinding to a checkpoint.
There is no per-allocation free.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct s_hugepage {
	struct s_hugepage  *next;
	struct s_hugepage  *prev;
	t_size             page_size;
	t_size             total;
	t_size             used;
	t_u8               data[];
} t_hugepage;

typedef struct s_arena {
	t_hugepage  *current;
} t_arena;

typedef struct s_checkpoint {
	t_size      used;
	t_hugepage  *location;
} t_arena_checkpoint;
.Ed
.Pp
Each page is one mapping of
.Fa page_size
bytes that begins with its
.Vt t_hugepage
header.
.Fa total
is the number of usable bytes in
.Fa data
and
.Fa used
is how many of them have been handed out.
Pages form a doubly linked list;
.Fa current
is the page allocations are served from.
Pages after
.Fa current
are left over from a rewind and are kept for reuse.
.Pp
.Fn xft_new_arena
maps a first page of
.Dv HUGEPAGE_256MB
bytes and commits only its header.
.Pp
.Fn xft_arena_alloc
returns
.Fa size
bytes aligned to
.Fa align
from the current page and commits them.
.Fa align
must be a nonzero power of two,
.Fa size
must be nonzero, and
.Fa size
must not exceed
.Dv HUGEPAGE_16GB
minus
.Ql sizeof(t_hugepage)
minus
.Fa align
\- 1.
If the current page has no room, the arena moves to the next page.
A page already following
.Fa current
is reused, with
.Fa used
reset to 0, when its
.Fa page_size
is large enough; otherwise a new page is mapped and linked directly after
.Fa current .
The new page size is
.Fa size
+
.Fa align
\- 1 plus the header, rounded up to 256 MiB, 512 MiB, 1 GiB, 2 GiB or 16 GiB.
The unused tail of the page being left is not handed out again until a
rewind returns to it.
Memory from a freshly mapped page reads as zero; memory reused after a
rewind keeps its previous contents.
.Pp
.Fn xft_arena_checkpoint
records the current page and its
.Fa used
count.
It does not allocate and is declared
.Ql pure .
.Pp
.Fn xft_arena_rewind
makes the checkpointed page current again and restores its
.Fa used
count.
Everything allocated after the checkpoint becomes invalid.
Pages that were in use after the checkpoint stay mapped and linked after
.Fa current ,
so later allocations reuse them without new mappings.
.Pp
.Fn xft_arena_rewind_clean
does the same, then unmaps every page after the restored current page.
.Pp
.Fn xft_destroy_arena
unmaps every page of the arena, forward and backward from
.Fa current ,
and sets
.Fa current
to
.Dv nullptr .
Calling it again on the same arena does nothing.
.Pp
To use an arena through the
.Vt t_allocator
interface, see
.Fn xft_arena_allocator
in
.Xr xft_alloc 3 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_new_arena
returns the new arena.
If the first mapping fails it returns an arena whose
.Fa current
is
.Dv nullptr .
.Pp
.Fn xft_arena_alloc
returns a pointer to the block, or
.Dv nullptr
if the request is invalid or a mapping or commit fails.
.Pp
.Fn xft_arena_checkpoint
returns the checkpoint.
.Pp
.Fn xft_destroy_arena ,
.Fn xft_arena_rewind
and
.Fn xft_arena_rewind_clean
return nothing.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_result	scratch_loop(t_arena *arena, t_size rounds)
{
	t_arena_checkpoint	cp;
	t_u8				*scratch;

	while (rounds--)
	{
		cp = xft_arena_checkpoint(arena);
		scratch = xft_arena_alloc(arena, 4096, 64);
		if (scratch == nullptr)
			return (KO);
		xft_memset(scratch, 0, 4096);
		xft_arena_rewind(arena, cp);
	}
	return (OK);
}

t_result	run(void)
{
	t_arena		arena;
	t_result	res;

	arena = xft_new_arena();
	if (arena.current == nullptr)
		return (KO);
	res = scratch_loop(&arena, 100);
	xft_destroy_arena(&arena);
	return (res);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_alloc 3 ,
.Xr xft_gpa 3 ,
.Xr xft_palloc 3 ,
.Xr xft_syscalls 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
An arena whose
.Fa current
is
.Dv nullptr ,
as returned by a failed
.Fn xft_new_arena ,
must not be passed to
.Fn xft_arena_alloc ,
.Fn xft_arena_checkpoint
or the rewind functions; they dereference it without checking.
.Pp
Checkpoints are only valid in stack order.
After
.Fn xft_arena_rewind_clean
to a checkpoint, any checkpoint taken after it may point to an unmapped page.
.Pp
Every page reserves at least 256 MiB of address space.
On Linux the mappings are plain private anonymous mappings, so physical
memory is only used for touched pages, but the reservation still counts
against strict overcommit limits.
